{
  "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUIRED",
  "summary": "The audit of the post-transactIssue live-close omission is correct, recovery already reconciles the exact row, and the three in-memory halt/close/fold checks cover both bind orderings without a new store contract. Frozen RED/GREEN acceptance still names an exact D.110c-0c1b refusal token while forbidding a new product class, which cannot be surfaced by the three v3-live.ts checks.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "P1",
      "title": "Exact 0c1b refusal token cannot be produced by frozen GREEN",
      "evidence": "docs/production-hardening/production-hardening-tdd-plan-v2.md D.110c-0c1b (RED expects earlier close to refuse with exact D110C_0C1B_COMMITTED_ISSUANCE_RECOVERY_REQUIRED, then says the token is tests-only and existing fail-closed kinds/details stay unchanged); packages/node/src/v3-live.ts creatorCloseRegistration()/stageSnapshot() (undefined mint; all fold failures mapped to snapshotExportFailure(\"not-active\", detail)); packages/node/src/creator-close.ts bindCreatorLiveClose()/close() (undefined registration → CREATOR_CLOSE_UNAVAILABLE; snapshot failure → TypeError `creator snapshot export failed: ${snapshot.kind}` and detail is discarded). GREEN is frozen to three internal v3-live.ts checks and must not edit creator-close.ts or add a public result kind.",
      "impact": "A RED transcribed like the sibling 0c1a `rejects.toThrow(TOKEN)` cannot go green under the frozen repair, so correct halt/close/fold code fails. The two orderings already surface different existing errors, so inventing a product 0c1b string would also breach the no-new-API freeze.",
      "required_action": "Keep D110C_0C1B_COMMITTED_ISSUANCE_RECOVERY_REQUIRED as the test-owned case id only. Freeze product-visible refusals: bind-after-failure returns existing CREATOR_CLOSE_UNAVAILABLE; pre-bound/in-flight close fails the existing snapshot-export path after the fold recheck. Do not add a new public kind, wire field, or creator-close.ts mapping."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "Defective first close needs a prior in-graph issued prefix",
      "evidence": "packages/node/src/internal/creator-issuance-retirement-boundary.ts deriveCreatorIssuanceRetirementBoundary(): unadmitted suffix truncates, but admitted === null throws D110C_0C1A_RETIREMENT_CHECKPOINT_UNAVAILABLE. A sole committed-but-omitted issue is an empty admitted prefix, so the queued close cannot advance.",
      "impact": "A minimal RED that only fires the one-use journal failure can fail first-close for the 0c1a empty-prefix reason and mis-attribute causality.",
      "required_action": "Disposition on the RED owner: compose the witness onto a genuine closeable D.110c-a prefix (at least one in-graph issued vertex) so the defective close truncates rather than hitting admitted === null. No executable-scope change."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "Halt reuse newly fail-closes close after received-path halt",
      "evidence": "packages/node/src/v3-live.ts handleV3Ingress() already sets operationAdmissionHalted on post-durable received journal/graph/policy failures when a reservation exists; creatorCloseRegistration()/stageClosedBlueprintEpoch() currently ignore it. GREEN checks 2–3 would refuse creator close on that existing flag too.",
      "impact": "Broader live-session close refusal after received uncertain outcomes, not only local committed issuance. Fail-closed and does not drop a local issued row.",
      "required_action": "Disposition: treat as intended conservative reuse of the existing recovery-required flag; do not carve a received-path exception in this slice."
    }
  ],
  "red_causal": true,
  "green_closes_red": false,
  "scope_preserved": true
}
{
  "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUIRED",
  "summary": "Audit custody and hashes verify exactly; the demonstrated post-commit defect, queue-ordering causality, recovery-owner claim (Q1: correct — recovery reconciles the exact row once, no schema/API/wire/dependency needed), the three-check coverage of both close orderings (Q2), operationAdmissionHalted as owner (Q3: correct for committedFailure paths; terminal ambiguity is separately fail-closed via terminalState), RED causality through the real issue/store/close path with a one-use journal adapter (Q4), and GREEN acceptance of exact-once recovery, policy reconstruction, 0→1→2 continuation and non-null retirement boundary (Q5) all hold. One material failure ordering is missing (Q6): a non-terminal transactIssue throw classified ISSUANCE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN — a contract-sanctioned possibly-committed outcome — sets no flag when no operation policy is installed, so the same close-omits-durable-row defect survives the frozen GREEN on no-policy planes. The fix is a small extension of the same seam, not a redesign.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "P1",
      "title": "Ambiguous transactIssue outcome (ISSUANCE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN) escapes unconditional halt on no-policy planes",
      "evidence": "packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:5682-5699 (catch sets operationAdmissionHalted only via `else if (reservation !== undefined)` when signerResolved && !terminalTransactionStarted); packages/issuance-store/src/terminal.ts:111-126 (classifyDurableIssuanceTerminalSuppression throws UnknownOutcomeFailure when the commit may have landed but readback is unreadable, and returns the commit when durable rows prove success at :153-163); packages/storage-browser/src/internal/browser-issuance-store.ts:748-776 (real ambiguous-throw path); packages/storage-node/src/internal/node-issuance-store.ts:801-819 (committing-failure readback path). Plan GREEN item 1 (docs/.../production-hardening-tdd-plan-v2.md:96241-96243) is scoped to 'after issuer.issue() returned' and the fault matrix (:96294-96298) lists only committedFailure positions; 'failure before journal write' denotes post-commit pre-journal committedFailure cases (v3-live.ts:5711-5760), not a pre-return ambiguous transaction throw.",
      "impact": "On a creator plane without an operation admission policy, a transactIssue throw with ISSUANCE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN can leave a durable issued/pending-outbox row while the issue reports 'issuance-rejected: v3 local issue transaction failed', no recovery-required flag is set, and neither close check (items 2-3) fires — the queued or later close folds a graph omitting the durable row, reproducing the exact demonstrated defect (omitted orphan row freezes later closes per the D.110c-0c1a derivation) after GREEN passes. Incorrect code therefore passes the frozen acceptance for a supported no-policy configuration.",
      "required_action": "Amend GREEN item 1 so the existing flag is also set unconditionally when the non-terminal issue transaction outcome is ambiguous: bind the caught error at v3-live.ts:5682 and set registration.operationAdmissionHalted whenever the thrown error carries code ISSUANCE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, independent of reservation presence (do not flag the definitely-pre-commit capacityRejected throw, which never reaches the store commit). Add 'issuance transaction outcome unknown (non-terminal, no policy)' to the focused fault matrix with the same close-refusal and recover-exactly-once assertions. No new schema, API, wire field, dependency, or store operation is required; the RED journal-rejection witness remains causal and unchanged."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "Unconditional flag set silently widens halt to ingress and no-policy issue paths",
      "evidence": "v3-live.ts:3727 (handleV3Ingress rejects new admissions when operationAdmissionHalted) and :5600-5602 (issueOneVertex rejects); GREEN item 1 makes the flag reachable for planes without an operation policy for the first time, so post-commit failures on such planes now halt all subsequent ingress and local issue until deactivate+recovery — a behavioral consequence the plan text does not enumerate (it discusses only close refusal).",
      "impact": "A retained suite that exercises a post-commit failure without a policy and expects continued ingress/issue could newly fail; conversely the broadening is fail-closed and consistent with the flag's existing meaning, so no incorrect close can pass.",
      "required_action": "Owner: GREEN implementer. Disposition: no plan-text change required; the frozen retained gates (E5-01 admission/recovery, D.110c-a/b hot path, issuance/outbox suites) are the check — if any retained test depends on post-failure continuation without a policy, that is exactly the plan's stated stop-and-reslice trigger rather than a silent pass."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "Fold-time refusal leaves blueprintClosing latched; acceptance should pin the in-memory wedge",
      "evidence": "v3-live.ts:6713 sets registration.blueprintClosing = true synchronously before the queued recheck in stageClosedBlueprintEpoch would refuse; the only in-memory clearing is abortSnapshotStage (v3-live.ts:6991-6996), and with the flag set every subsequent creatorCloseRegistration refuses, so the plane remains open-but-unclosable until deactivation plus recovery creates a fresh registration (v3-live.ts:7263-7310 with operationAdmissionHalted: false).",
      "impact": "Intended fail-closed behavior, and the GREEN text's 'neither refusal may change durable head, snapshot, graph, application state, or the surviving row' is not violated (the latch is in-memory only); but an acceptance test could accidentally assume the refusal unwinds blueprintClosing or consumes the close claim (it does not — claimedCreatorCloseHandles is only marked on a successful mint, v3-live.ts:2590-2591).",
      "required_action": "Owner: GREEN test author. Disposition: assert explicitly that the refused close leaves durable state untouched, that the close claim is not consumed by a refused mint (post-recovery close on the fresh registration succeeds — already implied by the 0→1 continuation), and that abortSnapshotStage remains the only in-memory abort; no production-code or plan-scope change."
    }
  ],
  "red_causal": true,
  "green_closes_red": false,
  "scope_preserved": true
}
{
  "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUIRED",
  "summary": "The causal attribution, the choice of recoverV3LiveReplica() as the sole reconciliation owner, the no-new-marker/schema/API conclusion, and the choke-point placement of the fold recheck in stageClosedBlueprintEpoch() are all correct and verified against source: only recoverV3LiveReplica() mints a RecoveredV3Live capability (v3-live.ts:5322), so a fresh registration with a clear flag genuinely requires the authenticated outbox/journal scan (v3-live.ts:5155-5199), and stageBlueprintEpoch() sets blueprintClosing synchronously and queues the fold behind the in-flight issue (v3-live.ts:6713-6714), making the fold-time recheck sufficient for both bind orderings on the paths the plan names. One blocking gap remains: the freeze scopes GREEN to committedFailure() only, but issuer.issue() can also *reject* after the durable commit — the genuine node store COMMITs (node-issuance-store.ts:802) and then throws UnknownOutcomeFailure from its readback (terminal.ts:126) — and that arm (v3-live.ts:5682-5699) still marks recovery-required only when an operation-policy reservation exists, which is precisely the no-policy configuration GREEN step 1 exists to fix. An implementation matching the freeze would satisfy the frozen acceptance while the demonstrated defect survives under that ordering, which is also absent from the fault matrix. RED is causal and feasible as written; two P2s are dispositioned to the implementation owner.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "P1",
      "title": "Post-commit issuer.issue() rejection is outside the frozen GREEN, so the defect survives with no policy installed",
      "evidence": "Plan lines 96239-96248 scope step 1 to \"after issuer.issue() returned, every committedFailure()\"; audit.md:9-13 asserts \"All failures from committedFailure() therefore occur after durable issuance\". But packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:5682-5699 is a second post-durable arm: node-issuance-store.ts:802 executes COMMIT, then line 819 calls #classifyAfterTerminalSuppression(); if the readback fails (lines 829-838 -> observation {unreadable:true}) packages/issuance-store/src/terminal.ts:126 throws UnknownOutcomeFailure, so transactIssue() rejects after the issued+outbox rows are durably committed. In that arm v3-live.ts:5685-5687 sets operationAdmissionHalted only when `reservation !== undefined`, and reserveOperation() returns undefined whenever no policy is installed (v3-live.ts:4150). For a non-terminal issue terminalTransactionStarted is false, so line 5698 returns admission-rejected with the flag clear. The frozen fault matrix (plan lines 96294-96298) lists \"journal write then thrown outcome\" but no issuance-transaction-rejects-after-commit ordering.",
      "impact": "In a deployment with no operation admission policy - exactly the configuration step 1 is written to cover - a durably committed issued/pending-outbox row leaves operationAdmissionHalted false, so neither the bind-time refusal (step 2) nor the queued fold recheck (step 3) fires. creatorCloseRegistration() mints close authority and stageClosedBlueprintEpoch() folds a graph omitting the row, reproducing the demonstrated defect; after adoption that row still carries the old epoch/anchor and freezes later closes with D110C_0C1A_RETIREMENT_CHECKPOINT_UNAVAILABLE. Because the ordering is not in the acceptance matrix, an incomplete GREEN passes.",
      "required_action": "Extend GREEN step 1 from \"every committedFailure()\" to \"every failure that is not provably pre-durable\": at v3-live.ts:5685-5687 drop only the `reservation !== undefined` condition, keeping the existing `!signerResolved && !terminalTransactionStarted` release discriminator so the retained E5-01 gate \"releases a definitely pre-issuance failure and permits one clean retry\" (tests/e5-01-v3-operation-admission-red.test.ts:172-204, signer rejects before resolving) stays green. Add one fault case to the RED/GREEN matrix: the issuance transaction rejects with an unknown outcome after its durable commit, with no operation admission policy installed, and the close must still refuse. No schema, API, wire field, or store change is implied."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "Reusing operationAdmissionHalted widens the halt and refusal domain beyond local issuance without acceptance coverage",
      "evidence": "packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:3727 refuses every non-duplicate received vertex while the flag is set, and the ingress path itself sets the same flag at lines 3803, 3813, 3827, 3834, 3840 and 3853. Unconditional marking therefore newly halts gossip admission in no-policy deployments after a post-commit local-issue failure, and conversely a received-vertex journal/graph failure will now refuse creator close under steps 2 and 3. The frozen acceptance (plan lines 96281-96292) states only that neither refusal may change durable head, snapshot, graph, application state, or the surviving row.",
      "impact": "A behavior change outside the stated close-refusal scope ships unstated: an ingress-side halt can block a close that would previously have proceeded, and a local post-commit failure now stops received-vertex admission. Both are fail-closed and defensible, but they are untested and could surface as an unexplained retained-gate failure at GREEN.",
      "required_action": "Owner: D.110c-0c1b implementation. State both consequences explicitly in the acceptance text and pin them with one assertion in the retained D.110c-a/b hot path (a plane with no injected failure still binds and closes). No executable scope change beyond that assertion."
    },
    {
      "severity": "P2",
      "title": "blueprintClosing stays latched after a step-3 fold refusal and is not covered by the refusal-state assertions",
      "evidence": "packages/node/src/v3-live.ts:6713 sets registration.blueprintClosing = true synchronously before the fold is queued at 6714; a step-3 recheck failure returns a fold failure and stageSnapshot() maps it to snapshotExportFailure (7029) without resetting the flag. issueLocal() (5842) and authenticateV3Ingress() (3888) then refuse for the life of the registration, and only abortSnapshotStage() (6991-6996) clears it.",
      "impact": "After a correct refusal the plane is left latched closed in memory. This is harmless under the plan's deactivate-then-recover remedy, but the frozen acceptance does not assert the post-refusal in-memory state, so either behavior (latched or reset) would pass and the eventual choice is unrecorded.",
      "required_action": "Owner: D.110c-0c1b implementation. Assert the intended post-refusal registration state in GREEN - either the refusal leaves blueprintClosing latched and recovery is the only exit, or step 3 resets it - and record the choice in the plan text. No new product surface."
    }
  ],
  "red_causal": true,
  "green_closes_red": false,
  "scope_preserved": true
}

